{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>If a man steal an ox or a sheep, and slaughter it or sell it.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): for transgressions that occur more frequently, it is fitting to increase the punishment. And since the Torah obligated the thief to pay double restitution, therefore for stealing small livestock — which are commonly found outside the town, in fields and wildernesses — it obligates him to pay double the double, and this is: ״וארבע צאן תחת השה״ — and four sheep in place of the sheep. Likewise, stealing of large livestock is more common, because it is impossible for the shepherd to drive them before him as with small livestock; therefore the Torah was stricter in their theft and added one more, saying ״חמשה בקר ישלם תחת השור״ — five cattle shall he pay in place of the ox. Thus wrote the Rambam, of blessed memory." "<b>But</b> our Rabbis, of blessed memory, explained for us two reasons in this matter. They said that what the punishment for stealing an ox was increased beyond that of the sheep is because the thief carried the sheep on his shoulder, thereby suffering disgrace in doing so — unlike the ox, which walked on its own legs. And from this they derived how great is the dignity of creatures, that it pushes aside a negative commandment in the Torah: the Holy One, blessed be He, has regard for the dignity of His creatures. And in the case of the ox, which was made idle from its labor — unlike the sheep — the Scripture was stricter concerning it; and from this they derived how great is labor. And as for why the Torah obligated the thief to pay twofold while the robber pays only the principal, our Rabbis, of blessed memory, already gave a reason: because the robber equated the servant to his master, but the thief accorded more honor to the servant than to his master, as it is said (Isaiah 29): ״הוי המעמיקים מה' לסתיר עצה והיה במחשך מעשיהם ויאמרו מי ראנו ומי יודענו״, and it is written (Psalms 94): ״ויאמרו לא יראה יה וגו׳״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״If a man steal an ox or a sheep, and slaughter it or sell it״ — because we stole an ox and made a calf, therefore ״five cattle shall he pay in place of the ox״: we repaid for it in that our fathers died on its account in the wilderness; and ״four sheep in place of the sheep״ — in place of the fact that we stole Joseph, that is, we were enslaved in Egypt for four hundred years, and four kingdoms ruled over us. Thus far the language of the Midrash. And the statement that our fathers died on its account in the wilderness — it appears that it mentions this regarding those who were killed by the hand of the sons of Levi, who were about three thousand men, and likewise in the plague by the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He. But because it mentions the number five, it seems to me that we received five punishments on its account, in place of the ox, and they are: killing by the sword at the hand of the sons of Levi; plague at the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Exodus 32): ״ויגוף ה' את העם״; the draining of the face by Moses, who intended to test them as suspected adulteresses (sotah); the destruction of the Holy Temple, may it be rebuilt speedily in our days, as it is said (Ezekiel 9): ״קרבו פקודות העיר״ — and the term פקודות corresponds to the expression used here (Exodus 32): ״וביום פקדי ופקדתי״; and therefore it says פקודות, a remembrance for the generations, for even though the Holy One, blessed be He, does not exact punishment for the sin, it is nonetheless remembered in every generation; and in the verse ״פוקד עון אבות״ (Exodus 34), I shall explain this, with the help of the blessed Name."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "Citation reads ישעיה כט (=Isaiah 29) and תהלים צד (=Psalms 94), both normalized to standard chapter numbering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:57.388422343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}